    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Now that you’re 15 years old, adulthood is in the near future, along with many more life decisions. One of them is whether to learn to drive. Traditionally, getting a driver’s licence is seen as a major step toward adulthood. Does this mean you should sign up for driving lessons as soon as you turn 18? How could having a car change your life?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h1",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Living with a car"
          }
        ],
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Cars offer a level of convenience that’s hard to beat. But this convenience comes with a price tag. You may know a bit about this if your family owns a car. Beyond the car itself, there are costs for gas (or electricity), "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "insurance",
            "ref": "v-insurance"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", parking, repairs, and sometimes even speeding tickets."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But these financial costs aren’t the end of the story. In the world’s busiest cities, drivers can spend nearly 100 hours per year stuck in traffic on their way to work. And in some places, you’ll have to circle the block several times, like a vulture ("
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "vulture",
            "zh": "兀鹫"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "), to find a parking space. One study found that the "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "average",
            "ref": "v-average"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " New Yorker spends 107 hours a year looking for parking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h2",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Living without a car"
          }
        ],
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Without a car, everyday tasks like shopping can be quite tiring. No matter how strong your biceps ("
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "biceps",
            "zh": "二头肌"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ") are, you can only carry a limited number of shopping bags at a time. This means more trips to the store or extra delivery fees. And let’s not forget those stormy summer days and freezing winter mornings. They can make shopping and many other daily chores even harder if you don’t have a car."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But living car-free has its advantages. Getting around on foot or by bike helps you stay fit. It also lets you explore your city up close. Without being boxed in by car windows, you’re more likely to spot something heart-warming on the street. Believe it or not, these little moments can brighten your day!"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
